Conventions: `70h` is hexadecimal; multi-byte values on the wire are
little-endian; parameters are numbered p0, p1...; slot numbers on the wire
count from zero (disk slot 1 is `p0 = 0`).

## The hardware

| Target | Processor | Host link |
|---|---|---|
| `fujinet-rs232-rev0` | ESP32, 8 MB | UART 1 via TRS3238E: RX GPIO 13, TX GPIO 21 |
| `fujinet-rs232-s3` | ESP32-S3, 16 MB | UART 1 via TRS3238E: RX GPIO 41, TX GPIO 42 |
| `fujiversal-rs232` | ESP32-S3 | USB CDC-ACM host to a Fujiversal cartridge; no UART, baud ignored |

The serial boards are **DCE** with a female DE9 (straight onto a PC COM
port). Only TXD (pin 3, in), RXD (pin 2, out) and ground are needed: commands
are entirely in-band. The firmware requests RTS/CTS flow control but never
routes the pins to the UART; CTS is simply held on. 8N1, **115200 baud** by
default (`[Serial] baud`).

**RI (pin 9)** is an attention line: held on normally, it toggles off/on every
`2 x rate` ms (rate 100 ms by default, SET_INT_RATE) while any network unit has
data, has lost its connection or has an error. Polling STATUS is always an
alternative. fujinet-pc drives the PC's DTR or RTS instead (`[Serial] proceed`).

At power-up the boot image named by `[General] boot_mode` (mode 0:
`/autorun.img`) sits in disk slot 1. With `configenabled=0` the listed disk
slots are mounted as soon as WiFi is up; with `1` (default) a program must send
MOUNT_ALL or MOUNT_IMAGE.

**fujinet-pc** (the firmware built for a PC, `cmake -DFUJINET_TARGET=RS232`)
speaks the same packets over a serial port (`[Serial] port`) or a TCP socket
(`[BOIP] enabled=1`, port 1985): `./fujinet -c fnconfig.ini -s SD -u http://0.0.0.0:8000`.

## fnconfig.ini

Read at start from `/fnconfig.ini` on the SD card if present, else flash; saved
to flash then SD (`fnconfig_on_spifs=1`) or SD only (`0`). On fujinet-pc, the
`-c` file. Sections match by case-insensitive prefix; numbered sections count
from 1; yes/no values are true if they start with `1 T t Y y` (`[WiFi]
enabled`, `multi_ap` and `[Bluetooth] enabled` need exactly `1`).

| Section / key | Default | Meaning on RS-232 |
|---|---|---|
| `[General] devicename` | `FujiNet` | network host name |
| `[General] timezone` | empty (UTC) | POSIX TZ for the clock |
| `[General] configenabled` | `1` | `0` = mount listed slots at start-up |
| `[General] altconfigfile` | empty | if set, slots are not auto-mounted |
| `[General] boot_mode` | `0` | boot image in slot 1: 0 autorun, 1 mount-and-boot, 2 lobby, 3 hisioboot |
| `[General] printer_enabled` | `1` | `0` = printer ignores everything, no reply |
| `[General] encrypt_passphrase` | `0` | obfuscate the stored WiFi passphrase |
| `[WiFi] enabled`, `SSID`, `passphrase`, `multi_ap` | `1`, -, -, `0` | the network; `[WiFiStored1-8]` hold more |
| `[HostN] type`, `name` | | `SD` or `TNFS`, and the server name |
| `[MountN] hostslot`, `path`, `mode` | | host slot **from 1**, path, `r`/`w` |
| `[PrinterN] type`, `port` | `1` | emulation (see the printer); **only `[Printer1]` works** |
| `[Serial] baud` | `115200` | line speed |
| `[Serial] port` | empty | fujinet-pc: the serial device |
| `[Serial] proceed` | `DTR` | fujinet-pc: line carrying RI (`DTR`, `RTS`, `none`); **lost on save** |
| `[BOIP] enabled`, `host`, `port` | `0`, -, `1985` | carry the bus over TCP |
| `[Modem] modem_enabled`, `sniffer_enabled` | `1`, `0` | the modem device |
| `[PhonebookN] number`, `host`, `port` | | modem phonebook |
| `[Network] sntpserver` | `pool.ntp.org` | time server |
| `[S3]`, `[GoogleDrive]`, `[OneDrive]` | | cloud credentials for the `N:` protocols |

`[Bluetooth]`, `[Cassette]`, `[CPM]`, `[ENABLE]` are ignored on RS-232;
`[Tape1]` overwrites the first disk slot (bug) - leave it out. Example for an
Altair at 9600 baud:

```ini
[General]
devicename=ALTAIR-FN
configenabled=0
[WiFi]
SSID=My Network
passphrase=my password
[Serial]
baud=9600
[Host1]
type=SD
name=SD
[Host2]
type=TNFS
name=tnfs.fujinet.online
[Mount1]
hostslot=2
path=/cpm/altair/cpm22.dsk
mode=r
```

## The FujiBus packet

One packet from the computer, one back. GET_WIFISTATUS:

{{ex:first}}

**SLIP framing:** `C0`, the packet with `C0` sent as `DB DC` and `DB` as
`DB DD`, `C0`. Send exactly one END each side (a leading double END loses the
command). Bytes before the first END go to the modem.

{{ex:esc}}

**Header** (after unescaping):

| Offset | Field | |
|---|---|---|
| 0 | device | who it is for; in a reply, who answered |
| 1 | command | in a reply: `06` ACK or `15` NAK |
| 2-3 | length | total packet length **including the header**, LE, before escaping |
| 4 | checksum | see below |
| 5 | descriptor | first parameter descriptor |

**Checksum:** 8-bit sum of the whole packet with end-around carry, computed
with byte 4 as zero, not inverted.

```c
uint16_t chk = 0;
for (i = 0; i < len; i++) { chk += buf[i]; chk = (chk >> 8) + (chk & 0xFF); }
```

In Z80: `ADD A,(HL)` then `ADC A,0`.

**Descriptors** (low 3 bits): `0` none, `1`-`4` that many 8-bit params, `5`
one 16-bit, `6` two 16-bit, `7` one 32-bit. Bit 7 = another descriptor byte
follows the header (before the values). The firmware reads values, not widths.
**Send every parameter a command reads** - a missing one crashes the firmware.

{{ex:multi}}

**Payload:** everything after the parameters. Fixed-size structures (256-byte
paths, the 97-byte SSID block...) must be sent **at full size**, zero-padded.

**Reply:** device = who answered, command `06` ACK (data in the payload) or
`15` NAK (never data), descriptor `00`.

{{ex:fuji.bad_command}}

## Transactions

- One command at a time; write data rides in the command, read data in the
  reply.
- Within a frame the firmware waits 200 ms per byte (500 ms over TCP);
  send packets without pausing.
- Before the reply, allow seconds: network OPEN (DNS/TLS), SET_SSID (tens of
  seconds), MOUNT_HOST and directories on TNFS, MOUNT_IMAGE, COPY_FILE.
- **No reply at all** for: bad checksum/length, stalled frame, RESET, printer
  disabled, missing parameter (crash), anything to the modem.
- An unknown device gets a NAK with device byte `00`:

{{ex:unknown_dev}}

- Reads of state and settings are safe to retry; network READ/WRITE, printer
  WRITE and the hash/Base64/QR input commands are not.
- Drain stale input before each command (SET_SSID sends **two** NAKs on a
  failed join).

**RESEND (where supported).** fujinet-pc branch `rs232-resend` answers command
`05` to any device by re-sending its last reply byte for byte, without
re-running the command. Use it only when part of a reply arrived damaged.
Firmware `670b2ed55` NAKs it:

{{ex:resend.nak}}

## The devices

| Device | Name | |
|---|---|---|
| `70` | Fuji | the adapter: WiFi, hosts, slots, directories, app keys, helpers |
| `31`-`38` | Disk | disk slots 1-8 |
| `71`-`78` | Network | the eight `N:` units |
| `45` | Clock | the time, many formats |
| `40` | Printer | renders to a file on the adapter |
| `50` | Modem | Hayes-style modem dialling TCP |
| `5A` | CP/M | defined but **not on the RS-232 bus** |

## The Fuji device (70h)

| Code | Command | Params / payload | Reply |
|---|---|---|---|
| `FA` | GET_WIFISTATUS | - | 1: `03` up, `06` down |
| `EA` | GET_WIFI_ENABLED | - | 1 |
| `FD` | SCAN_NETWORKS | - | 1: count |
| `FC` | GET_SCAN_RESULT | p0 index | 34: SSID (33), RSSI (signed dBm) |
| `FB` | SET_SSID | 97: SSID (33) + password (64) | ACK when joined; two NAKs on failure |
| `FE` | GET_SSID | - | 97 - **includes the stored password** |
| `E8` | GET_ADAPTERCONFIG | - | 140: SSID 0, hostname 33, IP 97, gateway 101, netmask 105, DNS 109, MAC 113, BSSID 119, version 125 |
| `C4` | GET_ADAPTERCONFIG_EXTENDED | - | 240: the 140 + addresses as text at 140, 156, 172, 188, MAC 204, BSSID 222 |
| `F4` | READ_HOST_SLOTS | - | 256: 8 x 32 names |
| `F3` | WRITE_HOST_SLOTS | 256 | ACK; all hosts unmounted |
| `F9` | MOUNT_HOST | p0 host | ACK |
| `E6` | UNMOUNT_HOST | p0 host | ACK (NAK if never mounted; NAK for SD on fujinet-pc) |
| `E1` | SET_HOST_PREFIX | p0 host; 256 prefix | ACK |
| `E0` | GET_HOST_PREFIX | p0 host | 256 (leftover memory after the NUL) |
| `F7` | OPEN_DIRECTORY | p0 host; 256: path NUL [pattern NUL] | ACK |
| `F6` | READ_DIR_ENTRY | p0 length, p1 flags (`80` details, `C0`+ block mode) | p0 bytes; `7F 7F` at the end |
| `F5` | CLOSE_DIRECTORY | - | ACK |
| `E5` / `E4` | GET / SET_DIRECTORY_POSITION | - / p0 (16-bit) | 2 / ACK |
| `F2` | READ_DEVICE_SLOTS | - | 304: 8 x (host, mode, name 36) - **names only, no paths** |
| `F1` | WRITE_DEVICE_SLOTS | 304 | ACK (writing READ's reply back loses the paths) |
| `E2` | SET_DEVICE_FULLPATH | p0 disk, p1 host, p2 mode (1 R, 2 RW); 256 path | ACK |
| `DA` | GET_DEVICE_FULLPATH | p0 disk | 256 |
| `F8` | MOUNT_IMAGE | p0 disk, p1 mode | ACK |
| `E9` | UNMOUNT_IMAGE | p0 disk | ACK |
| `D7` | MOUNT_ALL | - | ACK |
| `E7` | NEW_DISK | 262 | **always NAK** (after truncating the file) |
| `D9` | CONFIG_BOOT | p0 | ACK |
| `D6` | SET_BOOT_MODE | p0 mode | ACK |
| `FF` | RESET | - | none (restarts) |
| `D8` | COPY_FILE | p0, p1 hosts **from 1**; `src\|dst` NUL | ACK |
| `DC` | OPEN_APPKEY | 6: creator (16), app, key, mode (0 R, 1 W), 0 | ACK |
| `DE` | WRITE_APPKEY | up to 64 bytes | ACK |
| `DD` | READ_APPKEY | - | length (16) + data; 64 zeroes + ACK on failure |
| `DB` | CLOSE_APPKEY | - | ACK |
| `D0`/`CF`/`CE`/`CD` | BASE64 encode input/compute/length/output | p0 count (16) + data / - / - / p0 count | ACK / ACK / 4 (counts a NUL) / data |
| `CC`/`CB`/`CA`/`C9` | BASE64 decode, the same | | |
| `C8` | HASH_INPUT | p0 count (16) + data | ACK |
| `C7` / `C3` | HASH_COMPUTE (/NO_CLEAR) | p0: 0 MD5, 1 SHA1, 2 SHA256, 3 SHA512 | ACK |
| `C6` / `C5` | HASH_LENGTH / OUTPUT | p0 `01` hex, `00` binary | 1 / digest |
| `C2` | HASH_CLEAR | - | ACK |
| `BC` | QRCODE_INPUT | p0 count (16) + text | ACK |
| `BD` | QRCODE_ENCODE | p0 version, p1 ECC (0-3), p2 shorten | ACK (NAK if too small) |
| `BE` | QRCODE_LENGTH | p0 form: 0 binary, 1 ANSI, 2 bitmap, 3 SVG, 4 ATASCII, 5 PETSCII | 4 |
| `BF` | QRCODE_OUTPUT | p0 count (16) | data |
| `D3` | RANDOM_NUMBER | - | 4 |
| `BB` | GENERATE_GUID | - | 37 |
| `53` | STATUS | - | 4 zeroes |
| `00` | DEVICE_READY | - | 512 x `41` |

